#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116156} :activity/id #uuid "a4c1d15d-a43d-51de-bc2d-191d98aa4e53" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050676 :topic/id #uuid "5e0ed384-c5d0-57c1-be41-97361492eb5d" :topic/math-academy-id 1586} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116506 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. How can we plot the graph of $y=f(2x)$?\n\n![](images/1a/1a11bef66d0529418dde00e5713ed5e98f53a948c8a60f83fd056fd9e2c18698.png)\n\nWe can use some points on the graph of $y=f(x)$ to find the corresponding points on the graphs of $y=f(2x)$.\n\nFor example, let's take the point $(-2,0)$. The fact that it lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$ means\n\n$$\nf(-2)=0\n$$\n\nThen, if\n\n$$\nx={\\color{red}-1}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nf(2x) = f(2\\cdot({\\color{red}-1})) = f(-2) = 0\n$$\n\nThis means the point $({\\color{red}-2},0)$ that lies on $y=f(x)$ has a corresponding point $({\\color{red}-1},0)$ that lies on $y=f(2x)$.\n\nSo, for each point on the graph of $y=f(x)$, there will be a point on the graph of $y=f(2x)$ with the *same* $y$-value and *one half* the $x$-value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Points on [math]y=f(x)[/math] & Points on [math]y=f({\\color{red}2}x)[/math] \\\\ (-2, 0) & (-1, 0) \\\\ (0, 2) & (0, 2) \\\\ (1, 2) & (0.5, 2) \\\\ (4,-3) & (2,-3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can plot the points and draw the graph of\n\n$$\ny = f(2x){:}\n$$\n\n![](images/5d/5df5e404864825a7236bc553a401cb83d43ad00d67706845e90872aca4fa5d12.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(2x)$ is the graph of $y=f(x)$ **horizontally scaled** by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, we can say that the original graph is **compressed** by a scale factor of $2$.\n\nIn general, the transformation $y=f(kx)$ scales the graph of $y=f(x)$ horizontally by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{k}\n$$\n\n- If $k$ is greater than $1$, the graph is compressed (or squished) towards the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $k$.\n- If $k$ is between $0$ and $1$, the graph is stretched away from the $y$-axis by a scale factor of $k$." :tutorial/id #uuid "0f9522aa-685f-5492-a439-69c534d5bbef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3285 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c0285e1d-13e9-58ab-bba4-cfc15f23ca69" :step/math-academy-id 18031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115264 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115093 :question/id #uuid "57ece1a0-70e5-523f-8f6d-73a2fef43492" :question/math-academy-id "e-4983"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd783d1a-745d-5cd7-a5c3-9a109b6d1350" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115520}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Graph of a Horizontally Stretched Function"} :step/id #uuid "f833b0bd-73c5-5268-9213-0c6017d44609" :step/math-academy-id 18032 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115265 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115094 :question/id #uuid "9da96071-1bed-5e17-9d59-45948a0aaa5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4984"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d199ed2c-8188-5d80-9bb6-7b69ae684747" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048838}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115523}] :knowledge-point/title "Plotting the Graph of a Horizontally Compressed Function"} :step/id #uuid "e73f013f-5338-5d6d-9d71-6eed3918d6ff" :step/math-academy-id 18034 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115266 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115095 :question/id #uuid "0063a610-8c9a-587f-bd0a-7296d778059b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4985"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03cffed8-0c7f-5175-907c-3285cc07b2ec" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115953}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point that Lies on a Horizontally Stretched Function"} :step/id #uuid "708280f8-6071-589c-9998-87930ecb29a9" :step/math-academy-id 18035}] :activity/title "Horizontal Stretches of Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115420}]}
